# Example: Form Validation with Testing

## Goal Statement

```
/goal build contact form with email validation,
until form validates email format and submits to /api/contact endpoint,
verified by testing form with valid and invalid emails confirming appropriate behavior,
while don't modify the existing page header or layout,
or stop after 15 turns
```

## Breakdown

### Task
"build contact form with email validation"
- Specific work to do
- Includes both form building AND validation

### Finish Line
"until form validates email format and submits to /api/contact endpoint"
- Concrete, measurable outcome
- Two specific requirements: validation + submission
- Can be verified without ambiguity

### Verification
"tested by testing form with valid and invalid emails confirming appropriate behavior"
- Manual testing approach
- Tests both success and failure paths
- Specific: valid email (should submit) and invalid email (should show error)
- Can be done without a test suite

### Boundaries
"don't modify the existing page header or layout"
- Clear constraint on what to avoid
- Prevents scope creep to entire page redesign
- Allows focused work on just the form

### Limit
"or stop after 15 turns"
- Realistic for form implementation work
- Enough time to handle edge cases
- Prevents infinite refinement

## Real-World Variants

### With Automated Testing

```
/goal build contact form with email validation,
until form validates email and submits to /api/contact,
verified by running form tests with 10 valid and 10 invalid email addresses,
while don't modify page header or layout,
or stop after 20 turns
```

**Why this variant:**
- More rigorous
- Tests more email formats
- Prevents regression
- Automated, repeatable

### With Multiple Validation Fields

```
/goal build contact form,
until form validates email, phone, and required message field,
verified by testing form with empty fields (shows errors) and valid data (submits),
while don't modify page header or styling,
or stop after 20 turns
```

**Why this variant:**
- Defines all validation requirements upfront
- Tests both error and success paths
- More complete form functionality

### Multi-Phase: Build Then Enhance

**Phase 1:**
```
/goal build contact form,
until form accepts name, email, message and displays submission confirmation,
verified by filling form and confirming submission message displays,
while don't add complex validation beyond required fields,
or stop after 12 turns
```

**Phase 2:**
```
/goal add form validation,
until email is validated and message has character limit,
verified by testing invalid email (error shows) and long message (error shows),
while don't modify form layout,
or stop after 10 turns
```

**Why this variant:**
- Phase 1 focuses on basic functionality
- Phase 2 adds validation refinement
- Clear phase boundaries
- Easier to verify each phase

## Common Issues and Fixes

### Issue: Too Vague Finish Line

❌ **Bad:**
```
until form works and validation is good
```

✅ **Good:**
```
until form validates email format using regex and submits to /api/contact endpoint
```

---

### Issue: Missing Verification Details

❌ **Bad:**
```
verified by testing the form
```

✅ **Good:**
```
verified by testing with valid email (example@test.com), invalid email (invalid-email),
and empty email field, confirming appropriate messages display
```

---

### Issue: Boundaries Too Loose

❌ **Bad:**
```
while keeping it simple
```

✅ **Good:**
```
while don't modify the existing page layout or header styling,
and only add JavaScript for validation
```

---

## Testing Checklist

When verifying this goal:

**Valid Email Tests:**
- [ ] test@example.com (submits)
- [ ] user.name@company.co.uk (submits)
- [ ] first+last@domain.com (submits)

**Invalid Email Tests:**
- [ ] notanemail (shows error)
- [ ] @domain.com (shows error)
- [ ] user@ (shows error)

**Edge Cases:**
- [ ] Empty email field (shows error)
- [ ] Submit button disabled until form is valid
- [ ] Confirmation message displays after submission
- [ ] Form appears on the page without breaking existing layout
